module j3.term.screen
    A full-screen terminal UI front: raw mode, the alternate screen, a back buffer the app draws into, and a `flush`
    that sends only what changed. Linux (on other targets `open` is `Err`).
    match screen.Screen.open(&arena) {
    Result.Ok(s) {
    s.put(2, 1, "hello", style);  s.box(0, 0, 20, 5, style);
    s.flush();                    // one write: only cells that differ from the last frame
    s.poll(50, &events);          // keys, paste, mouse, focus, and EV_RESIZE
    s.close();
    }
    Result.Err(msg) { ... }
    }
    Drawing never touches the terminal: `put`, `fill`, `box`, `clear` edit the back buffer (clipped to the screen and the
    `clipPush` rect), `flush` diffs it against what the terminal shows. After a resize or the first frame the whole screen
    is redrawn. `openFds(inFd, outFd, cols, rows, arena)` runs on pipes (no raw mode unless `inFd` is a tty): the bytes
    `flush` writes replay into a `j3.term.grid.Grid` to the same screen, which is how the tests check it.
    Exit safety: `open` registers a restore with `sys.onExit`, so the tty is put back (cursor, alt screen, paste/mouse
    modes, cooked mode) when the program exits, returns from `main` or panics (the panic message prints after). One real
    Screen at a time: a second `open` takes over the registration. Limits: only the exit paths are covered, a fatal
    signal (SIGTERM, SIGHUP, SIGKILL) skips the restore, and Ctrl+C arrives as a key because raw mode turns ISIG off.
    Resizes are noticed when `poll` runs, so a blocking `poll(ms)` can take up to `ms` to report one. Main thread.

const EV_RESIZE = 5
    `Event.kind` of a resize seen by `poll`: `x` is the new column count, `y` the rows. (`j3.term.input` uses 1 to 4.)

struct Screen
    inFd: int
    outFd: int
    ncols: int
    nrows: int
    cap: int
        Cells allocated per buffer (a resize within it does not allocate).
    front: []Cell
        What the terminal shows, and what the app drew.
    back: []Cell
    full: bool
        Redraw everything on the next flush.
    cx0: int
        Clip rect `[cx0, cx1) x [cy0, cy1)` and the saved rects under it.
    cy0: int
    cx1: int
    cy1: int
    clips: []int
    nclips: int
    curX: int
        Where the app wants the cursor.
    curY: int
    curVis: bool
    pen: Style
        What the terminal is known to be doing: pen, cursor position (when `posKnown`), cursor visibility.
    posX: int
    posY: int
    posKnown: bool
    termVis: bool
    mouse: bool
    closed: bool
    eof: bool
        Input ended (EOF on `inFd`).
    backlog: bool
        An event was held back (after a paste); the next `poll` does not wait.
    isTty: bool
    out: Text
    dec: Decoder
    inBuf: []u8
    lastBytes: int
        Bytes the last `flush` wrote.
    arena: &Arena
    fn Screen.open(arena: &Arena): Result<Screen, string>
        The real terminal: stdin and stdout, at the terminal's size (80x24 when it cannot say).
    fn Screen.openFds(inFd: int, outFd: int, cols: int, rows: int, arena: &Arena): Result<Screen, string>
        Read keys from `inFd`, write frames to `outFd`, `cols` x `rows` cells. Raw mode only when `inFd` is a tty.
        `Err` when raw mode fails or the target has no terminal support.
    fn Screen.enableMouse(this: &Screen)
        Report mouse clicks, drags and wheel (SGR encoding) as `EV_MOUSE` events. Undone by `close`.
    fn Screen.close(this: &Screen)
        Put the terminal back: mouse and paste modes off, cursor shown, main screen, cooked mode. Idempotent.
    fn Screen.cols(this: &Screen): int
    fn Screen.rows(this: &Screen): int
    fn Screen.cell(this: &Screen, x: int, y: int): Cell
        The cell the app drew at `(x, y)` (blank outside the screen).
    fn Screen.resize(this: &Screen, cols: int, rows: int)
        Change the screen size: both buffers blank, the clip reset, the next flush a full redraw. `poll` calls it when
        the terminal's size changed; call it directly for pipes. Storage grows only past the largest size so far.
    fn Screen.poll(this: &Screen, ms: int, events: &List<Event>): int
        Wait up to `ms` for input and append events to `events` (cleared first): keys, paste (its `text` is valid until
        the next `poll`, and a paste is the last event of its call), mouse, focus, and `EV_RESIZE` when the terminal's
        size changed (the screen is already resized; redraw before `flush`). Returns the event count. Input at
        end-of-file sets `eof`.
    fn Screen.repair(this: &Screen, i: int, x: int)
        Blank the cell next to a wide char that a write at index `i` (column `x`) is about to cut in half.
    fn Screen.setCell(this: &Screen, x: int, y: int, cp: i32, w: int, style: Style)
        Store a cell of width `w` (1 or 2) at an in-bounds, unclipped place.
    fn Screen.put(this: &Screen, x: int, y: int, s: string, style: Style): int
        Draw the UTF-8 text `s` at `(x, y)` in `style`: wide characters take two columns, combining marks and control
        bytes are skipped, cells outside the screen or the clip rect are not drawn (a wide character cut by the clip is
        dropped). No wrapping. Returns the columns the text spans, drawn or not, so callers can lay out the next piece.
    fn Screen.fill(this: &Screen, x: int, y: int, w: int, h: int, cp: i32, style: Style)
        Fill the rect with `cp` (0 or 32 for blanks) in `style`.
    fn Screen.box(this: &Screen, x: int, y: int, w: int, h: int, style: Style)
        A single-line box: its outer edge is the `w` x `h` rect at `(x, y)` (at least 2 x 2); the inside is untouched.
    fn Screen.clear(this: &Screen)
        Blank the whole back buffer (plain style), ignoring the clip.
    fn Screen.clipPush(this: &Screen, x: int, y: int, w: int, h: int)
        Restrict drawing to the rect (intersected with the current clip) until `clipPop`. Up to 16 levels deep.
    fn Screen.clipPop(this: &Screen)
        Undo the last `clipPush` (no-op when there is none).
    fn Screen.setCursor(this: &Screen, x: int, y: int, visible: bool)
        Where the terminal cursor goes at the next flush, and whether it is shown (it starts hidden).
    fn Screen.quiet(this: &Screen)
        Hide the cursor before the first thing a frame draws (shown again at the end if the app wants it).
    fn Screen.moveTo(this: &Screen, x: int, y: int)
        Emit the shortest cursor move to `(x, y)`: nothing when already there, CUF along a row, else CUP.
    fn Screen.flush(this: &Screen): bool
        Send what changed since the last flush with one `write` (nothing when nothing changed). A frame after a resize,
        and the first frame, clear the screen and draw everything. False when the write failed.

